## Errors & Mistakes
The initial lint run reported `ST1005: error strings should not be capitalized` for the API status error; changed its prefix to lowercase. A compiled check found that SIGTERM did not interrupt a synchronous stdin read. The regression failed with `cancellation did not interrupt stdin` before the fix. An initial native signal recheck used a 200 ms delay and returned signal status -2; replacing the delay with a pipe-write handshake made the check wait until the process was reading. Six default-date request cases failed because due_on was absent before the default was corrected. One planner check used an incomplete file path and reported that the plan was not found; the corrected check passed.

The previous local merge used a cached origin/master without fetching its latest state, so the next push reported `[rejected] (fetch first)`. An initial instruction-file read used a Zsh loop variable named path, which altered PATH for that subprocess and produced `zsh:4: command not found: cat` and `zsh:7: command not found: cat`; a fresh subprocess read the files correctly. Automatic command review rejected the first rebase command before execution because its temporary-file cleanup contained `rm -f style commands are not permitted. Use a safer approach`. The same work completed with ordinary rm and no force flag.
## Dead Ends
A synchronous io.ReadAll could not respond to cancellation while stdin stayed open. The final implementation reads through a buffered result channel and selects on the command context. A timed delay was not a reliable synchronization mechanism for native signal tests, so those checks use enough input to require an active reader. No API name resolution, workspace discovery, caching, or retry layer was added.

